The slopes match the regression run by hand above. The signature has no
argument for returns, by design, so the Euler equation stays a test
rather than becoming a target. The dictionary that comes back is
exactly what a model takes, so
`lrr.LongRunRisksModel(claims=lrr.calibrate_claims(dc, {...}))` goes
from cash-flow series to a model that is ready to solve in one line.

For steps 3 and 4, put both measured loadings into one model and solve
it. Everything except the loading is held at the same values as the two
synthetic firms above, so the only difference between the rows is the
measured cash-flow exposure.

The work comes in three layers, and the order matters. First you
calibrate each claim from its own cash flows. Second you put the claims
in a model, which prices all of them in one economy. Third, once the
model is solved, you pick two of the priced claims and compare them,
which is the `compare` call below. The third step stands apart because
it asks a different kind of question. The model prices claims. Which
pair of claims makes a spread worth reporting is your question, not the
model's, and one solved model answers it for any pair you name:

::: {#6dcbd3ba .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
legs = lrr.LongRunRisksModel(
    claims={"value leg": dict(BASE, phi=phi["Value"]),
            "growth leg": dict(BASE, phi=phi["Growth"])},
).solve(method="analytical")

spread = legs.compare("value leg", "growth leg")
print(f"Value-growth spread from long-run risks: "
      f"{spread.premium:.2f} points")

leg_names = list(legs.claims)
(
    GT(pd.DataFrame({
        "leg": leg_names,
        "phi": [legs.params.claims[n].phi for n in leg_names],
        "a1": [legs.A1[n] for n in leg_names],
        "prem": [legs.long_run_premium[n] for n in leg_names],
    }))
    .tab_header(title="The two legs priced from measured loadings")
    .cols_label(leg="", phi="phi", a1="A1", prem="long_run_premium %")
    .fmt_number(columns="phi", decimals=2)
    .fmt_number(columns="a1", decimals=1)
    .fmt_number(columns="prem", decimals=2)
    .tab_options(**KIKU_GT)
)
```

